//! The orphan rule and the social parents (ADR-0018 section 4, design note of the
//! households, part 2): a dependent child without a fit carer goes, with its siblings of
//! the household, to the nearest living relative of 18 or more, else to the foster mother
//! `FOSTER_RANK` draws among the women of 18 or more in an open partnership, else into a
//! household of its own; a relative and the foster parents become its social parents, a
//! biological parent or a step-parent does not.
//!
//! The expected bytes come from `tools/state_reference.py`, which runs the model of
//! `tools/world_reference.py`, written from the design note and not from this code. Each
//! scenario runs on the test data of "clan" (accident 0.02 per year, the old fertility
//! window) with its separation rate, seed 0xff, the switch off, reference alleles only:
//!
//! - "kin" (`test:kin9`, separation 0.025, 32 years): a mother dies and her three children
//!   go to a foster mother (no relative of 18 or more yet), who and whose partner become
//!   their social parents; a couple separates while two adult children still live at
//!   home (they stay, without a carer); the father dies and his adult daughter at home
//!   takes over her six younger siblings without a move; she leaves home and the rule
//!   chooses her again, so the five who are still dependent follow her.
//! - "step" (`test:step`, the window from 16, separation 0.05, 14 years, two inputs): a
//!   mother of 17 keeps her child; she pairs anew, dies, and her partner cares on as
//!   step-father (no social parent); he pairs anew, the children move with him; he
//!   separates and they go with him; he dies and the two children, without a relative
//!   of 18 or more or a foster mother, get a household of their own.
//! - "neither" (`test:neither17`, the data of "homes", 34 years, one input): a mother dies
//!   and her four children go to their aunt in another household, their social parent; at
//!   a later separation in the aunt's household the children she cares for get the woman of
//!   the couple as carer (cared for by neither partner, they stay with her) and the adults
//!   at home stay.

